# What do economists actually agree on about markets?
voices: {"a":"gemini:Achird | curious, open-minded economist host who loves a puzzle; warm, thinks aloud, brisk lively pace (never slow, never rushed); takes objections seriously, never preachy","b":"gemini:Kore | sharp, friendly co-host: asks what a smart listener would, pushes back with real objections, sometimes explains; dry wit, never exaggerated; brisk lively pace, never slow"}
prompt: "What do surveys of economists show? Where does the agreement end?"
next: {"title":"Do markets work? Putting it together","href":"https://www.drawcast.app/#gh=hmelberg/drawcast-library/econ-from-zero/69-do-markets-work-putting-it-together.cast"}

## Where they agree
use: bar_chart
with:
    labels:
      - Tariffs hurt
      - Immigrants help
      - Antitrust
      - 'Min. wage: jobs'
      - Pollution tax
    values:
      - 95
      - 97
      - 93
      - 65
      - 88
    value_labels: true
    bar_colors: same
    colors:
      - null
      - null
      - null
      - orange
      - null
    ylim:
      - 0
      - 100
    y_label: Agree (%)
    reference:
      value: 67
      label: ⅔
      color: grey
    label_size: 26
    box:
      x: 60
      'y': 80
      w: 880
      h: 540
sources:
    - id: gs2024
      title: Consensus among economists 2020 — A sharpening of the picture
      authors: Doris Geide-Stevenson and Alvaro La Parra-Pérez
      year: 2024
      finding: 'Online survey of AEA members, Dec 2020 – Apr 2021 (1,770 replies). Share agreeing or agreeing with provisos: tariffs and import quotas usually reduce general economic welfare 95% (95% in 1990); immigration has a net positive economic effect for the US 97%; antitrust laws should be enforced vigorously 93%; pollution taxes or marketable permits more efficient than emission standards 88%; a minimum wage increases unemployment among young and unskilled workers 65%. Consensus criteria include at least two-thirds agreeing (or disagreeing). Journal of Economic Education 55(4): 461–478.'

    text joke "5 economists, 6 opinions?" at.percent [50,60] font_size 44 hidden true
    text tv "for  vs  against" #8f887c at.percent [50,42] font_size 32 hidden true
    text who "AEA members, 2020–21" #8f887c at.percent [84,99] cites ["gs2024"] font_size 24 hidden true

    card title "Where they agree"

What do economists actually agree on about markets? The old joke says nothing: ask five economists, get six opinions.
    draw joke

B: And on television they do come in pairs, one for and one against.
    draw tv

    erase joke tv

So ask them all. Over the winter of 2020, nearly eighteen hundred members of the American Economic Association answered the same list of statements.
    draw axes who

Each bar is the share who agreed, at leas (@draw bar_1@)t with provisos. Tariffs and import quotas usually make a country worse off: ninety-five percent.

B: Ninety-five. On trade, that's close to everyone.
    highlight bar_1

Immigr (@draw bar_2@)ation is a net gain for the US economy: ninety-seven.  (@draw bar_3@)Antitrust laws should be enforced vigorously: ninety-three.

    ask "Drag the pollution bar to your guess." on bar_5 store g default "50" voice b
        intro: Now pollution. A tax on it, or permits firms can trade, against plain rules on how much each firm may emit. How many economists say the price is the cheaper way to cut it?
        right: Close: eighty-eight percent.
        wrong: You said {g}; it is eighty-eight percent.

Eighty-eight percent. A price lets the firms that can cut cheaply do most of the cutting.
    highlight bar_5

The survey's authors count  (@draw reference_1@)a statement as consensus only when at least two in three agree.

B: Then here's one to test the line: a minimum wage raises unemployment among young and unskilled workers.
    highlight reference_1

Sixty-five percent. Just under the line.
    draw bar_4

    highlight bar_4 effect circle

    pause 0.4

So on how markets respond to a tariff, a price or a tax on pollution, the six opinions turn out to be nearly one,
    highlight bar_1 bar_2 bar_3 bar_5

and the minimum wage is where the chorus starts to break.
    highlight bar_4 effect pulse

## Where it ends
use: line_chart
with:
    x:
      - 1990
      - 2000
      - 2011
      - 2020
    series:
      - {name: Tariffs hurt, values: [95, 94, 95, 95]}
      - {name: Min. wage costs jobs, values: [82, 73, 74, 65]}
      - {name: Incomes more equal, values: [68, 68, 77, 86]}
    points: true
    ylim:
      - 0
      - 105
    x_label: Survey year
    y_label: Agree (%)
sources:
    - id: gs2024
      title: Consensus among economists 2020 — A sharpening of the picture
      authors: Doris Geide-Stevenson and Alvaro La Parra-Pérez
      year: 2024
      finding: 'Table 1, surveys of AEA members in 1990 (N=464), 2000 (298), 2011 (568) and 2020–21 (1,422), share agreeing or agreeing with provisos: tariffs and import quotas usually reduce general economic welfare 95, 94, 95, 95; a minimum wage increases unemployment among young and unskilled workers 82, 73, 74, 65; the distribution of income in the US should be more equal 68, 68, 77, 86. Journal of Economic Education 55(4): 461–478.'
    - id: ck1994
      title: 'Minimum Wages and Employment: A Case Study of the Fast-Food Industry in New Jersey and Pennsylvania'
      authors: David Card and Alan B. Krueger
      year: 1994
      finding: 'After New Jersey raised its minimum wage in April 1992, employment at its fast-food restaurants did not fall relative to restaurants in eastern Pennsylvania. American Economic Review 84(4): 772–793.'
    - id: igm2013
      title: Minimum Wage (US Economic Experts Panel, 26 February 2013)
      authors: IGM Forum / Kent A. Clark Center, Chicago Booth
      year: 2013
      finding: 'Raising the federal minimum wage to $9 would make it noticeably harder for low-skilled workers to find employment: 13 agreed, 12 disagreed, 9 uncertain. Its distortionary costs would be small enough relative to the benefits to make it desirable: 18 agreed (2 strongly), 4 disagreed (1 strongly), 12 uncertain.'
      url: https://kentclarkcenter.org/surveys/minimum-wage/

    scratch igm cites ["igm2013"] work ["$9 minimum (2013 panel)","cost jobs?  13 yes · 12 no · 9 unsure","worth it?  18 yes · 4 no · 12 unsure"] x 520 y 230 font_size 30 hidden true
    text w_cons "consensus" #5f8a52 at.data [2000,101] font_size 36 hidden true
    text w_open "open" #8a5fa8 at.data [1995,57] font_size 36 hidden true
    text w_cont "contested" #b5482e at.data [2015,52] font_size 36 hidden true

    card title "Where it ends"

B: One bar is a snapshot. The same statements have been put to economists four times since 1990, so we can watch them move.
    draw axes

B: Tariffs first: nin (@draw line_1@)ety-five percent in 1990, ninety-five in 2020. Thirty years, a flat line.

    ask "Draw the minimum-wage line on from 1990." on line_2 from 2000 store g default 80,80,80
        intro: And the minimum wage? It started at eighty-two percent in 1990. Draw where you think it went.
        right: Close: it slid to sixty-five.
        wrong: It slid further: sixty-five by 2020.

B: Down. Seventy-three, seventy-four, and sixty-five by 2020.
    highlight line_2

Did economists go soft, or did the evidence change?
    point at.data [2000,73] gesture circle

B: The evidence. In 1994 David Card and Alan Krueger compared fast-food jobs in New Jersey, which had raised its minimum wage, with Penns (@point at.data [2000,73] gesture tap@)ylvania n (@highlight line_2@)ext door, and found no loss of jobs.

That study was fought over for years, and the studies (@highlight line_2 effect pulse@) since still disagree on how big the job losses are.

B: You can see it in  (@draw igm_box igm_line_1 igm_line_2@)a 2013 panel of leading economists. Would a nine-dollar minimum make low-skilled jobs noticeably harder to find? Thirteen said yes, twelve no, nine weren't sure.

Change the question to whether it's (@draw igm_line_3@) worth it, costs against benefits, and the same panel tips: eighteen yes, four no.

    erase igm

B: Now a third line, ri (@draw line_3@)sing: the distribution of income in the US should be more equal. Sixty-eight percent in 1990, (@point at.data [2020,86] gesture circle@) eighty-six in 2020.

And no data set can settle that one. It's a judgement about fairness, not a finding about how markets work.
    highlight line_3

So three lines, three verdicts. Tariffs (@draw w_cons@) hurt: consensus.

The minimum wage's cost in (@draw w_cont@) jobs: contested.

How equal incomes should be: (@draw w_open@) open, a question of values.

    pause 0.5

## Economists and everyone else
use: bar_chart
with:
    labels:
      - Fuel tax
      - NAFTA helped
      - Buy American
      - Vouchers
    series:
      - {name: Economists, values: [93, 95, 11, 51]}
      - {name: Public, values: [23, 46, 76, 56]}
    value_labels: true
    ylim:
      - 0
      - 100
    y_label: Agree (%)
    box:
      x: 70
      'y': 110
      w: 570
      h: 520
    label_size: 26
sources:
    - id: sz2013
      title: Economic Experts versus Average Americans
      authors: Paola Sapienza and Luigi Zingales
      year: 2013
      finding: '19 policy questions put to the Chicago Booth IGM Economic Experts Panel and to about 1,000 Americans per wave (Financial Trust Index, Dec 2011 – Dec 2012). Agreement, experts vs public: carbon tax cheaper than car fuel-economy standards 92.5 vs 22.5; NAFTA increased average welfare 94.6 vs 46.2; ''Buy American'' raises manufacturing employment 11.4 vs 75.7; school vouchers 51.4 vs 56.3. Average gap 35 percentage points, largest where economists agree most. Told that nearly all experts prefer a carbon tax, public support moved from 23% to 26%. Of those favouring standards, 17% switched if promised a rebate; 51% of the rest did not trust the government to rebate. AER P&P 103(3): 636–642.'
      doi: 10.1257/aer.103.3.636
    - id: gs2024
      title: Consensus among economists 2020 — A sharpening of the picture
      authors: Doris Geide-Stevenson and Alvaro La Parra-Pérez
      year: 2024
      finding: 'Self-reported ideology of respondents (N=1,204): very liberal 9.1%, liberal 37.9%, moderate 42.0%, conservative 9.6%, very conservative 1.5%. Journal of Economic Education 55(4): 461–478.'
    - id: jc2023
      title: Who said or what said? Estimating ideological bias in views among economists
      authors: Mohsen Javdani and Ha-Joon Chang
      year: 2023
      finding: 'Online randomised experiment with 2,425 economists in 19 countries: agreement with the same statements fell when the attribution was changed from a mainstream to a less- or non-mainstream source, or removed. Cambridge Journal of Economics 47(2): 309–339.'
    - id: gd2013
      title: 'Views among Economists: Professional Consensus or Point-Counterpoint?'
      authors: Roger Gordon and Gordon B. Dahl
      year: 2013
      finding: 'On the IGM Economic Experts Panel: close to full consensus when the past literature on a question is large; no tendency for panelists from the same department, cohort, gender or PhD school to share views — no evidence of different camps. AER P&P 103(3): 629–635.'
      doi: 10.1257/aer.103.3.629
    - id: edinburgh2005
      title: Edinburgh congestion charge referendum, February 2005
      authors: City of Edinburgh Council (result as reported by BBC News)
      year: 2005
      finding: 133,678 No (74.4%) to 45,965 Yes (25.6%), turnout 61.7%; later research named lack of trust in the council as an underlying driver.
      url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Edinburgh_congestion_charge

    text gap_note "gap: 35 pts" #b5482e cites ["sz2013"] x 822 y 470 font_size 40 hidden true
    scratch told cites ["sz2013"] work ["told experts agree:","23% → 26%","half: no trust","in the payback"] x 822 y 470 font_size 28 hidden true
    scratch lean cites ["gs2024"] work ["economists, 2020:","47% liberal","11% conservative"] x 822 y 470 font_size 28 hidden true
    scratch bias cites ["jc2023"] work ["same words,","less mainstream","author:","less agreement"] x 822 y 470 font_size 28 hidden true
    scratch camps cites ["gd2013"] work ["Chicago panel:","no rival camps"] x 822 y 470 font_size 28 hidden true
    scratch recap work ["how markets respond:","consensus","thin evidence: split","what's fair: values"] x 822 y 470 font_size 28 hidden true
    text aboutwhat "about what?" #b5482e x 822 y 460 font_size 48 hidden true

    card title "Economists and everyone else"

Economists mostly agree with each other. But do they agree with everyone else? In 2011 and 2012 the same questions went to a panel of lea (@draw legend@)ding economists and to about a thousand ordinary Americans.
    draw axes

    ask "Which cuts carbon more cheaply: a tax on fuel, or fuel-economy rules for cars?" poll.choices [{"text":"A tax on fuel","share":0.23},{"text":"Not sure","share":0.14},{"text":"Rules for cars","share":0.63}] poll.source sz2013 store p after clear stage own voice b
        right: You and {p.share} of Americans chose {p}.

B: Pollution again. A tax on fuel, (@draw bar_1@) as a cheaper way to cut carbon than fuel-economy rules for cars: economists, ninety-three percent. The public: twenty-three.

NAFTA le (@draw bar_2@)ft the average American better off: ninety-five against forty-six. And ' (@draw bar_3@)Buy American' rules add factory jobs: economists eleven, the public seventy-six.

B: On school vouchers, where e (@draw bar_4@)conomists themselves are unsure, the two sit five points apart.

Across nineteen questions the gap averaged thir (@draw gap_note@)ty-five points, and it was widest exactly (@highlight bar_1 bar_2@) where economists agree most.

    erase gap_note

B: So the public just hasn't heard? Tell people  (@draw told_box told_line_1 told_line_2@)that nearly all experts prefer the carbon tax, and support goes from twenty-three percent to twenty-six.

Not ignorance, then. Promised the tax would come back to them as lower taxes, few changed their minds,  (@draw told_line_3 told_line_4@)and half of the rest said they didn't trust the government to pay it.

    erase told

B: But maybe the blind spot is the economists'. In the 2020  (@draw lean_box lean_line_1 lean_line_2 lean_line_3@)survey, forty-seven percent called themselves liberal and eleven percent conservative.

    erase lean

And in an experiment with eco (@draw bias_box bias_line_1 bias_line_2 bias_line_3 bias_line_4@)nomists in nineteen countries, the same statement won less agreement when it was credited to a less mainstream author.

    erase bias

B: Against that, on the Chicago panel Roger Go (@draw camps_box camps_line_1 camps_line_2@)rdon and Gordon Dahl found no rival camps, and near-full agreement wherever the research runs deep.

    erase camps

So what economists agree on i (@draw recap_box recap_line_1 recap_line_2@)s how markets respond: to tariffs, to prices, to a tax on pollution.

Where th (@draw recap_line_3@)e evidence is thin, they split;

where the questio (@draw recap_line_4@)n is what's fair, their survey answers are opinions like anyone's.

    quiz "A city asks economists whether raising its minimum wage from $12 to $13.50 over three years would noticeably cut jobs for teenagers. What would the answers most likely look like?" intro "Three new cases."
        right: A split, with many unsure: for a modest rise, the size of the job loss is exactly where the evidence is contested. A rise to double the local wage would be another matter.
        wrong: Is this about how all markets respond, or about the size of an effect the studies still fight over? The size: here, a split, with many unsure.
        * Near-unanimous yes
        + A split, with many unsure
        * Near-unanimous no

    quiz "Which of these statements could no survey of economists settle?" voice b
        right: The inheritance tax: whether it should be heavier is a judgement about fairness, not a finding.
        wrong: Two are claims about how markets respond; one is about what is fair: here, the inheritance tax.
        * Rent caps shrink the supply of rental housing
        * Pollution permits cut emissions at lower cost
        + Inheritances should be taxed more heavily

    quiz "In 2005 Edinburgh voted on a congestion charge, the kind of price on a harm economists favour: about three in four voters said no. What does the evidence here suggest is the main reason?"
        right: Doubt about the payback: people often grasp the trade-off but don't trust that the money will return to them.
        wrong: Telling people what the experts think barely moved them: here, doubt that the money comes back.
        * Voters didn't know what economists think
        + Voters doubted the money would come back to them
        * Voters don't understand prices

    erase recap

B: So next time someone says economists never agree, ask them: abou (@draw aboutwhat ends@)t what?

    pause 0.5

## Where next
